      "writtenOn": "2023-02-16T07:14:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "what is this?  Where is it documented?  Why isn\u0027t this type of feature submitted upstream first?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "b4de11fcccaea1aa88f9d41b6ea451291fb8695d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "341dd74c_421a5e0f",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1983635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-22T07:41:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I have submit a issue to descript this feature, please check the link: https://partnerissuetracker.corp.google.com/u/1/issues/255720038\n\nThe Android build system \nhas support for compiling C programs to eBPF, and Android includes an eBPF \nloader and library that loads eBPF programs at boot time, running \nuser-supplied eBPF programs can extend kernel functionality. These programs\ncan be hooked to probes or events in the kernel and used to collect useful \nkernel statistics, monitor, and debug. A program is loaded into the kernel \nusing the bpf(2) syscall and is provided by the user as a binary blob of \neBPF machine instructions. These eBPF machine instructions will be runtime\ncompiled to native code in kernel.\n\nSo, after the runtime compile eBPF bytecodes, the eBPF native code should\nbe protected, and if the eBPF is finished and free, then remove the protection.\nThis proteciton is implemented in secure-EL1, Hypervisor or ATF.",
      "parentUuid": "3de7dbb8_a1db8a86",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "b4de11fcccaea1aa88f9d41b6ea451291fb8695d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
